 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Provides that if the net balance of the reimbursement account exceeds two million five hundred thousand dollars, any amount over two million five hundred thousand dollars shall be transferred to the proprietary vocational school supervision account.",
 "title": "Relates to the transfer of certain funds from the licensed private career schools tuition reimbursement account to the proprietary vocational school supervision account",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A2235",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A2235-2023",
     "printNo": "A2235",
     "session": 2023,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 114\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 2235, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to amend the education law, in relation to the transfer of\n      certain funds from the licensed  private  career  schools  tuition\n      reimbursement  account to the proprietary vocational school super-\n      vision account\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would authorize the transfer of  all  funds  exceeding  $2.5\nmillion  contained  within  the  State's licensed private career schools\ntuition reimbursement  account  to  the  proprietary  vocational  school\nsupervision  account,  to  be  used  to fund operational expenses of the\nState Education  Department's  Bureau  of  Proprietary  School  Services\n(BPSS). The bill would make this procedure an ongoing transfer.\n \n  The  law  currently  requires  a  portion  of  the state assessment on\nlicensed private career schools in New York and  any  fines,  penalties,\nand  settlements  imposed  on  those schools to be placed into a tuition\nreimbursement account for the purpose of reimbursing students  in  situ-\nations  where  they  are  unable to complete classes due to the school's\nduties, fraudulent conduct, or  closure.  The  purpose  of  the  tuition\nreimbursement  account  assessment  is primarily to protect students: to\nmake them whole and protect them against potential loss.\n \n  These funds were collected for the purpose of benefitting students and\nnot to fund the operations of the BPSS. Repurposing funds originally set\naside for the protection of students to instead fund general  government\noperations is not appropriate.\n \n  Therefore, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n\n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
    "vetoNumber": 114,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2023
   }
